AB  - Home ventilators are essential devices for treating Obstructive Sleep Apnea (OSA), with Auto-Adjusting Positive Airway Pressure (APAP) algorithms serving as the embedded pressure-control mechanism. However, existing APAP algorithms usually exhibit asynchrony between pressure control and patient respiratory events. Such asynchrony can ultimately result in Patient- Ventilator Asynchrony (PVA). To address this issue, we propose Neural-Network-Enhanced Auto-Adjusting Positive Airway Pressure (N-APAP). N-APAP aims to model patient airflow data to guide pressure adjustments that stay synchronized with respiratory events, thereby mitigating PVA. We developed three teacher neural networks and obtained a student model via a cross-modal Knowledge Distillation (KD) strategy. The strategy employs temperature scaling, confidence weighting, and a two-stage weight-scheduling scheme. The student model achieves a coefficient of determination of approximately 0.971, with Flash usage of only 56.25 Kibibyte (KiB) and Random Access Memory (RAM) usage of only 3.04 KiB. A validation experiment on the Active Servo Lung 5000 (ASL5000) shows that N-APAP reduces treatment pressure by an average of 0.175 cm of water column (cmH2O) during respiratory events and 0.545 cmH2O during normal breath. N-APAP also maintains a Pearson correlation of 0.999 in inspiratory tidal volume compared with conventional APAP while treating. In comparison with APAP algorithms from two commercially ventilators, N-APAP is less prone to PVA and outperforms all other brands. N-APAP establishes a control paradigm enhanced by a neural network for low-computational-power devices, fills the application gap of neural networks in home ventilators, and provides a lightweight deployment solution for embedded medical systems.
