AB  - Sleep apnea (SA) is a chronic condition with wide-ranging effects, yet screening remains limited. SA detection using wearable smart wristband devices has the potential to significantly improve this situation. However, existing detection methods suffer from limited representation capacity and achieve low sensitivity. To this end, we propose an ordinal pattern similarity-guided supervised prototype contrastive learning (ops-spcl) framework with enhanced token selection module. Specifically, in the self-attention mechanism of the token selection module, we integrate a selective kernel convolution-ReLU operation. By incorporating receptive fields of varying scales, this approach enhances the model’s ability to focus more precisely on target-relevant token features, thereby improving the capture of temporal dependencies in SA events. Furthermore, considering the challenges of accurately capturing the features of SA events in the peak-to-peak interval sequences of mild to moderate patients, we design an ops-spcl loss function. This function explicitly constructs hard negative sample pairs and easy positive sample pairs to enhance intra-class compactness and inter-class separability that generates a more refined, effective, and discriminative feature space. On the wearable bracelet PPG dataset, the proposed model performs well in per-segment detection, achieving 72.4% sensitivity and a 0.687 F1-score. By analyzing SA detection rates across different population groups, we demonstrate that it effectively focuses on the hard samples of mild to moderate patients. Moreover, these findings highlight the generality of its strong generalization capabilities across multiple datasets. These results suggest that our approach holds significant potential for large-scale SA detection.
