AB  - Obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) is a common sleep disorder that immensely affects the quality of life. Currently, people go through polysomnography (PSG) for OSA diagnosis. There is a need for an alternate OSA identification approach since the current one is costly and time-consuming. In this research, an automated method for identifying OSA using a single-lead electrocardiogram (ECG) signal has been proposed. In total, 95 ECG recordings (70 recordings from the PhysioNet database and 25 recordings from the UCDSA database) are used to estimate the performance of the proposed model. Time domain, time–frequency domain, and nonlinear entropy-based features are extracted from these ECG recordings. A sequential forward feature selection (SFFS) algorithm is employed to select the best features contributing towards OSA identification. Various classifiers are used to measure the performance of the model, in which random forest (RF) classifier is the best performer with an accuracy of 93.17 ± 0.80 %, a precision of 91.66 ± 1.15 %, a sensitivity of 90.39 ± 1.21 %, a specificity of 94.90 ± 0.72 %, F1-score of 91.01 ± 1.06 %, MCC of 85.52 ± 1.70 %, and kappa of 85.51 ± 1.70 % for minute-by-minute on PhysioNet database. The results demonstrate that the combined time domain and entropy-based features are superior to other known automated OSA detection approaches in extracting hidden information from the ECG data. Our results also attest to the capability of single-lead ECG signals for efficient identification of OSA.
