AB  - Sleep apnea (SA) is a potentially serious yet often unnoticed sleep disorder characterized by repeated cessation and resumption of breathing. The noninvasive and real-time detection of SA is paramount in developing suitable therapeutic strategies. Recently, deep learning (DL)-based detection methods using electrocardiography (ECG) have demonstrated considerable potential and convenience compared to traditional expert-reliant methods. However, challenges persist: extracting multiscale SA-related features effectively from the ECG segment to be diagnosed, capturing varying-length transition features from the surrounding segments of the target one, and adaptively fusing the features of varying-length input segments. In this article, we propose a dual-multiscale interactive attention-based convolution neural network (DM-IACNN) for automatic SA detection using single-lead ECG signals. It composes a CNN-based interactive multiscale extraction (IMSE) module to learn the feature maps from ECG intrasegment. Furthermore, we propose a temporal-wise attention module to enhance the modeling of temporal features derived from the IMSE module. Finally, the proposed model accepts three segments of varying lengths obtained from adjacent ECG segments surrounding the target segment as multiscale inputs. It features a scale-wise attention module to fuse the temporal feature maps of these three segments, capturing the multiscale transition features between them. A series of experiments were conducted on the apnea-ECG dataset to investigate the effectiveness of the proposed DM-IACNN model. The results demonstrate that the DM-IACNN model delivers robust performance, with an accuracy of 91.02% and 100% of per-segment and per-recording evaluation paradigm, which outperforms the current state-of-the-art (SOTA) methodologies.
