AB  - Sleep apnea is a prevalent breathing disorder characterized by interruptions in airflow to the lungs lasting over 10 s, affecting nearly 1 billion people worldwide. It is a medically complex condition that necessitates prompt diagnosis for effective treatment. The gold standard approach, polysomnography (PSG), for detecting obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) is expensive and time-consuming. As a result, automated detection using multivariate signals has emerged as a widely studied area of research. Recent studies on ECG-based sleep apnea detection have concentrated on feature engineering techniques, utilizing these features as inputs for classification models. This study introduces a novel deep neural network to identify obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) from single-lead ECG signals with rigorous statistical validation. We applied continuous wavelet transform (CWT) to convert ECG signals into scalograms, extracted and selected features using statistical metrics to create a balanced dataset, then trained the proposed model (DREAM — Deep Residual ECG Apnea Model) to extract deep features. Experiments conducted on the Apnea-ECG database demonstrate that the proposed model achieves an accuracy of 99.93%, specificity of 98.93%, and sensitivity of 98.93% in per-segment classification. To enhance interpretability, gradient-weighted class activation mapping (Grad-CAM) is integrated to highlight key input regions contributing to predictions. Additionally, different classification architectures are investigated with standardized preprocessing and identical training conditions. The experimental results are competitive with, and in several comparisons exceed, existing ECG-based OSA detection approaches under identical settings.
