AB  - Detecting sleep apnea–hypopnea syndrome (SAHS) through polysomnography (PSG) is crucial for computer-assisted diagnostics. Although recent advancements in machine learning have enabled apnea–hypopnea (AH) event categorization via single-channel signals, imprecise AH event identification and duration metrics have hindered algorithmic progress. To achieve accurate detection of AH events, this paper proposes an AH event Detection Framework (AHDF) comprising a sample classification module (SCM) and an event segmentation module (ESM). The AHDF detects AH events from PSG by identifying positive samples and segmenting them. It then counts Apnea–Hypopnea Index (AHI) to screen for SAHS. In experiments, we examine the potential of nasal pressure and SpO signals for AH event detection. For the signal fragment classification by SCM, we achieved an accuracy of 90.72%, a sensitivity of 78.43%, a precision of 71.37%, a F1-score of 74.73% when compared to the result of Registered Polysomnography Technician (RPSGT), in the 39 collected cases. For AH event refine detection, we demonstrate the effectiveness of the ESM by comparing the labeling results with the visual interpretation results of RPSGT. We performed a consistency test between the AH event duration and AHI of the cases and their true values, respectively, and the correlation coefficients were 0.96 () and 0.95 (). According to the severity of SAHS defined by American Academy of Sleep Medicine (AASM) based on AHI (non-SAHS with AHI 5, mild with 5AHI15, moderate with 15AHI30, and severe with AHI 30), the AHDF achieved 82.05% accuracy in categorizing SAHS severity by calculating the AHI. The AHDF overcomes challenges in identifying AH event boundaries. Our comprehensive visualizations and statistical experiments demonstrate its practicality in assisting physicians in labeling AH events with the diagnosis of SAHS.
