AB  - Obstructive Sleep–Apnea (OSA) is a prevalent sleep disorder characterized by recurrent pauses in breathing during sleep and is recognized as a risk factor for cardiovascular morbidity. This paper proposes a lightweight and interpretable Cos-ECGNet model for detecting sleep apnea using single-lead electrocardiogram (ECG) data, which contains a cosine convolution block, an RRI-Amplitude fusion block, and a temporal feature extraction block to effectively capture the nonlinear dynamics inherent in ECG signals associated with apnea events. First, the raw ECG signals are preprocessed to obtain RR interval and amplitude features. Then, these features are fed into the proposed Cos-ECGNet model to identify apnea signals. Experimental results indicate that the proposed Cos-ECGNet achieves a competitive accuracy of 91.82 % on the publicly available Apnea-ECG dataset and 79.63 % on the UCDDB dataset with only 1186 learnable parameters, which are significantly fewer than existing models. Additionally, interpretability analysis reveals that Cos-ECGNet effectively captures the characteristic nonlinear signals associated with apnea events. The lightweight and interpretable design of the proposed model demonstrates its great feasibility for deployment in wearable devices, enabling sleep-apnea detection in home sleep tests.
