AB  - Sleep apnea syndrome (SAS) is a chronic respiratory disease that is extremely harmful to the human body. However, the existing SAS detection methods often exhibit high dependence on data labels, which cannot achieve high-accuracy diagnosis in the case of missing labels or insufficient label data. To this end, we proposed a multi-task self-supervised learning framework named one dimensional contrastive restoration neural network (1D-ConReNet) for SAS detection using single-lead electrocardiogram (ECG) signals. Specifically, utilizing two pretext tasks, signal transformations contrastive learning (CL) and signal restoration learning (SRL) to drive the neural network for pre-training. While obtaining generalized representation information via CL, SRL forces the neural network to learn fine-grained latent representations. In the fine-tuning phase, the ingenious design of 1D-ConReNet allows it to transfer weight information not only to generative adversarial network (GAN) but also to convolutional neural network (CNN), thereby simultaneously alleviating issues related to class imbalance and poor SAS detection performance under insufficient data annotation. The Apnea-ECG dataset is mainly utilized to conduct a comprehensive experimental evaluation of the proposed approach. The accuracy of 89.25%, the sensitivity of 88.41% and the specificity of 89.75% are achieved in the per-segment detection. The accuracy of 100.00%, the mean absolute error (MAE) of 3.57 and the correlation of 0.9541 are achieved in the per-recording screening. The results indicate that our 1D-ConReNet is effective in scenarios with limited labeled data. Furthermore, our approach provides a novel insight for the popularization of SAS daily monitoring.
