AB  - Obstructive Sleep Apnea-Hypopnea Syndrome (OSAHS) diagnostic approaches that analyze breathing sounds during sleep have great potential to develop a convenient home-based OSAHS monitoring system. However, deploying these diagnostic approaches on low-cost, resource-limited devices remains challenging, and most of the models developed in previous studies were based on private datasets. This work proposes a lightweight and easy-to-deploy OSAHS screening system that utilizes the publicly accessible PSG-Audio dataset. Specifically, the whole-night sleep breathing sounds signal is processed through a ConvNeXt + LSTM network with only 2.5 million parameters to capture the temporal patterns of breathing sounds associated with apnea-hypopnea events. Subsequently, the Apnea-Hypopnea Index (AHI) is estimated from these predictions using a linear regression model, enabling OSAHS screening. On an independent test set of 50 subjects, the estimated AHI showed a high correlation with PSG-observed AHI (Pearson correlation coefficient = 0.804, p < 0.001). When screening for severe OSAHS the proposed system achieved a sensitivity of 100 % and a specificity of 64.29 %. Assuming a 35 AHI cut-off point, the sensitivity and specificity were 97.14 % and 73.33 %, respectively. This lightweight system offers a practical solution on resource-constrained devices for prescreening and home management of OSAHS.
