AB  - Early detection and classification of sleep disorders are vital for public health. Polysomnography, the gold standard for diagnosing sleep apnea, is limited by its reliance on expert analysis and disruption of natural sleep. To overcome these limitations, we introduce SEHPNet, a novel non-invasive monitoring system that utilizes a spectrally enhanced hierarchical patch network to analyze a single respiratory signal. SEHPNet innovatively partitions the respiratory signal into multi-scale patch units and integrates spectral features, capturing both global and local time–frequency domain characteristics with high precision. In particular, the hierarchical patch embedding mechanism enables multi-scale analysis by dividing the respiratory signal into patches of varying lengths, thereby extracting both short-term fluctuations and long-term apnea trends. Through a self-attention mechanism, it effectively identifies periodic patterns and subtle respiratory events, offering a lightweight, high-accuracy, and minimally invasive solution for sleep apnea detection. Comprehensive evaluations demonstrate that SEHPNet achieves a remarkable 98.4% detection accuracy on the Apnea-ECG Database and robust generalization for binary and multiclass classification tasks on the MIT-BIH Polysomnographic Database. These results underscore SEHPNet’s potential to transform sleep disorder screening by leveraging a single respiratory signal with advanced signal processing and model architecture, paving the way for future advancements in non-invasive sleep monitoring technologies.
