AB  - Sleep apnea (SA) is a major global health issue, but existing diagnostic/predictive methods have flaws like reliance on static features, interference susceptibility, and poor real-time high-precision prediction. This study proposes a real-time SA prediction method using single-channel airflow signals and deterministic learning to enable effective SA event advance warning. Two dynamical features are used for prediction. Temporal dynamic trend features, which reflect long-term airway vulnerability changes, are compressed into an interpretable low-dimensional temporal factor to eliminate redundancy. Current dynamical features which capture short-term airway patency, are reconstructed into a two-dimensional matrix to mine hidden spatial topological features of SA precursors. They are integrated via a neural network with three components: a temporal feature compressor, multi-scale spatial feature extractor, and feature fusion classifier. Additionally, a novel advance warning sample generation mechanism is proposed, employing a sliding time window and consecutive-sample trigger rule to reduce false alarms. Experiments on three SA datasets and one ventilator dataset show the method predicts SA effectively 5 s in advance, with good generalization ability and a balanced prediction rate, missed report rate, and false alarm rate. The deterministic learning-based method with single-channel airflow signals realizes real-time SA advance prediction. It addresses existing method limitations by integrating long/short-term dynamical features and optimizing the warning mechanism, with clinical application potential for SA early warning and intervention.
