AB  - Sleep staging and obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) detection are essential to sleep medicine but remain constrained by the complexity of full polysomnography (PSG) and manual scoring. This article proposes a multimodal monitoring system that integrates EEG and ECG for automated sleep assessment. The system comprises two pipelines: SAGE-EEG, which extracts oscillatory and temporal descriptors through denoising, band decomposition, and spatio-temporal attention; and VITA-ECG, which leverages R-centered sub-epochs, variational mode decomposition, and adaptive feature weighting to capture autonomic dynamics. Both pipelines generate physiologically meaningful features that are encoded using lightweight neural modules and fused for final decision-making. Experiments on the DOD-H/O, ISRUC, and MASS datasets demonstrate robust sleep staging accuracies of 89.2%, 92.4%, and 93.8%, respectively, alongside reliable OSA risk assessment accuracies of 93.5%, 94.7%, and 95.9%. To support practical deployment, the framework is implemented as a clinician-oriented SleepCode Monitor enabling automated sleep staging and integrated OSA screening. While strong performance is observed across heterogeneous datasets and recording configurations, future work will focus on robustness to real-time sensor noise and hardware variability, as well as broader validation in clinical and home-based settings, particularly for patients with severe comorbidities and challenging sensor attachment conditions.
