AB  - Sleep Apnea (SA) is a common but serious breathing disorder that affects people of all ages, sex, and ethnicities worldwide. Given its widespread impact, there is a growing need for accurate and efficient diagnostic tools. Advances in deep learning (DL) and powerful computing systems have opened new possibilities for automated medical analysis, helping doctors detect SA faster. However, most current DL models depend on large and diverse datasets, carefully labeled datasets—which are difficult and time-consuming to prepare. To address this challenge, we developed a Semi-supervised Prototype-aware Hierarchical Embedding Matching (SPHE-Match) approach, which implements dynamic class-aware prototype mining for feature representation. Similarly, to refine pseudo-labels, a bi-level optimization approach is been utilized to correctly predict SA events using a single-lead Electrocardiogram (ECG) signal. This approach maximizes the utility of restricted annotated data while retaining high performance, reducing the need for expansive manual labeling. On the PhysioNet Apnea-ECG dataset, SPHE-Match delivered strong results, an achieved 93.24% accuracy. When evaluated on the MIT-BIH Polysomnographic database, SPHE-Match achieved an accuracy of 84.88%. This concludes how semi-supervised learning can improve SA classification, especially in resource-constrained environments.
