AB  - Early detection of obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) benefits individuals by improving overall quality of life and preventing severe complications associated with untreated OSA. Deep learning-based applications using single-lead electrocardiograms (ECG) have shown promise in automatic OSA detection. However, most efforts have not fully considered variations in the detected signals of breathing events. A breathing event is highly conditioned by the present context (individual characteristics, sleep position, and sleep stage), which varies over time. Additionally, the high variance among apnea events, associated with event duration, recovery period after apnea, and respiration pattern, should be utilized to support OSA detection. Leveraging these two characteristics, a temporal proximity contrastive learning (TP-CL) approach is proposed for OSA detection.
TP-CL consists of three modules: (1) a representation learning module using a DL-based encoder; (2) a temporal delta classification module that utilizes the difference between temporal proximities to support OSA detection; and (3) a temporal proximity contrast module employing a novel contrastive-based loss function to create a representation space with minimized influence of subject-variability and sleep context. Experimental results show that TP-CL achieves competitive results, outperforming state-of-the-art OSA detection models in both subject-dependent and subject-independent settings. A sensitivity analysis demonstrates the robustness of the proposed approach and provides insights into potential future applications of TP-CL. Additionally, TP-CL offers real-time detection capability utilizing the preceding and current ECG signals during testing. It is a promising tool for early OSA detection and in-home OSA monitoring.
